// =====================================================================
// Aurora Pharma cold chain — 02. INGESTION-TIME ENRICHMENT
//
// This is where a raw truck reading becomes a fact about a drug batch.
// Run after 01-schema.kql, before any data flows.
// =====================================================================

// --- 2.1 MANDATORY PRECONDITION -----------------------------------------
// The transform below uses `join`. Microsoft Fabric documents the restriction
// plainly (learn.microsoft.com/kusto/management/update-policy?view=microsoft-fabric,
// ms.date 2026-06-01, updated 2026-06-23):
//
//   "By default, the Streaming ingestion policy is enabled for all tables in
//    the Eventhouse. To use functions with the join operator in an update
//    policy, the streaming ingestion policy must be disabled."
//
// The engine reason (ADX streaming-ingestion limitations, ms.date 2026-02-10):
//   "The update policy can only reference newly ingested data in the source
//    table and not any other data or tables in the database"
//
// THE COST, and the article must state it: ingestion now follows the
// IngestionBatching policy, whose defaults are "5 minutes maximum delay time,
// 500 items, total size of 1 GB". So enrichment is batched, not instant.
// Use .alter-merge, not .alter. The alter-merge form is the one Microsoft
// documents for disabling ("The following command disables the streaming
// ingestion policy"), and it leaves any other policy properties intact.
.alter-merge table reefer_telemetry policy streamingingestion '{"IsEnabled": false}'

// Confirm it took. The streaming-ingestion command pages carry the
// azure-data-explorer moniker only, even though the Fabric update-policy page
// instructs you to run this in Fabric, so verify rather than assume.
.show table reefer_telemetry policy streamingingestion

// Optional: trade capacity for freshness. Documented minimum is 00:00:10.
// The docs warn that very low values raise cost, reduce performance, and can
// even INCREASE effective end-to-end latency.
// .alter-merge table reefer_telemetry policy ingestionbatching '{"MaximumBatchingTimeSpan": "00:01:00"}'


// --- 2.2 The transform ---------------------------------------------------
// Output schema must match excursion_events in column NAMES, TYPES and ORDER.
// The final project enforces that.
.create-or-alter function
with (
    docstring = 'Update-policy transform. Fans one telemetry reading out to every batch riding that truck on that leg, then scores it against that product\'s own safe band.',
    folder    = 'ColdChain'
)
ColdChain_EnrichExcursions()
{
    reefer_telemetry
    // kind=inner is REQUIRED, not stylistic. KQL's default join flavor is
    // innerunique, which DEDUPLICATES THE LEFT SIDE by the join key: it would
    // collapse every reading from a truck down to one row and silently destroy
    // the data. With kind=inner we get one output row per
    // (reading x matching leg_cargo row), which is the fan-out we want:
    // one reading -> every batch on that truck's current leg.
    | join kind=inner (
        leg_cargo
        | project LegID, TruckID, BatchID, MinSafeC, MaxSafeC, BudgetMinutes, DepartUtc, ArriveUtc
      ) on TruckID
    // Half-open window [DepartUtc, ArriveUtc) on purpose. KQL's `between` is
    // inclusive at BOTH ends, so on contiguous legs a reading landing exactly
    // on a handover timestamp would be counted against two legs.
    | where Timestamp >= DepartUtc and Timestamp < ArriveUtc
    | extend IsCold = TempC < MinSafeC
    | extend IsHot  = TempC > MaxSafeC
    | extend Direction        = case(IsCold, "cold", IsHot, "hot", "in-range")
    // The reading carries its own interval, so a 15-minute recorded reading and
    // a 30-second live reading are both scored correctly by the same code.
    | extend ExcursionMinutes = iff(IsCold or IsHot, IntervalMinutes, 0.0)
    // This reading's share of the product's budget. Computed HERE, at ingestion
    // time, because this is the only place that has both the minutes and the
    // budget in hand. It is what makes the materialized view in 03 possible:
    // a view can aggregate one table only, so it cannot reach leg_cargo for
    // the denominator. Since a percentage of a fixed budget is linear in the
    // minutes, summing these increments equals dividing the total.
    | extend BudgetPctIncrement =
        iff(BudgetMinutes > 0, 100.0 * ExcursionMinutes / todouble(BudgetMinutes), 0.0)
    // Column order below MUST equal excursion_events' column order. TruckID
    // resolves to the LEFT (telemetry) column; the join auto-renamed the right
    // side's copy to TruckID1, which is discarded here.
    | project BatchID, LegID, TruckID, Timestamp, TempC, ExcursionMinutes, Direction,
              BudgetMinutes, BudgetPctIncrement
}


// --- 2.3 Dry-run before attaching the policy -----------------------------
// The docs' own recommended technique: "You can test an update policy's
// performance impact by invoking the policy on already-existing extents,
// before creating or altering the policy." Run this AFTER some rows have
// landed and BEFORE 2.4. A broken transform blocks ingestion entirely.
//
// let _extentId = toscalar(
//     reefer_telemetry
//     | project ExtentId = extent_id(), IngestionTime = ingestion_time()
//     | where IngestionTime > ago(10m)
//     | top 1 by IngestionTime desc
//     | project ExtentId
// );
// let reefer_telemetry =
//     reefer_telemetry
//     | where ingestion_time() > ago(10m) and extent_id() == _extentId;
// ColdChain_EnrichExcursions
// | getschema


// --- 2.4 The update policy ----------------------------------------------
// .alter REPLACES the whole policy array. Use .alter-merge to add an entry
// alongside existing ones. Permission: Table Admin.
//
// IsTransactional:true — "In production systems, set IsTransactional:true to
//   ensure that the target table doesn't lose data in transient failures."
//   The trade: a bug in the transform now fails the RAW ingestion too. For a
//   regulated cold chain that is the right failure mode, because a batch that
//   silently loses its excursion rows is worse than an ingestion that stops
//   loudly.
// PropagateIngestionProperties:false — it only carries extent tags and
//   creation time, neither of which this pipeline uses.
// NO ManagedIdentity — that property exists in the Azure Data Explorer moniker
//   only. Fabric auto-populates a read-only OwnerPrincipalDetails instead.
.alter table excursion_events policy update
@'[{ "IsEnabled": true, "Source": "reefer_telemetry", "Query": "ColdChain_EnrichExcursions()", "IsTransactional": true, "PropagateIngestionProperties": false }]'

.show table excursion_events policy update


// --- 2.5 CATCH UP THE HISTORY — do not skip -------------------------------
// The update policy fires at INGESTION time, for rows that arrive after it is
// attached. It never reaches back. The seed backfill ran in Part 2, before
// this policy existed, so the whole recorded story (AUR-2207's 330 frozen
// minutes included) sits in reefer_telemetry unscored. Without this step,
// excursion_events holds only post-policy live rows and every check in
// 05-verify.kql fails silently.
//
// Run it ONCE, with the live stream STOPPED and its last queued batch drained
// (~5 min after interrupting the sender). The function reads the whole raw
// table when invoked directly, so with nothing arriving mid-command, every
// pre-policy row is scored exactly once and post-policy rows keep coming from
// the policy. Run it with the stream still sending and the overlap rows get
// scored twice.
.set-or-append excursion_events <|
    ColdChain_EnrichExcursions()
